=== AUDIT_EVIDENCE ===
Source: files/fleet-telemetry-audit/report.md (cited path above).

Headline numbers (verbatim from report.md "Summary"):
- Tesla Field enum entries: 260 total
- Actionable fields (excluding Deprecated_*, Experimental_*, Unknown): 241
- In SignalRegistry: 230 / 241        ← 11 actionable fields silently missing
- In HotCatalog (typed columns): 7
- In KnownColdSignals (signal_observations): 223
- Have NormalizeFleetUnits parser: 27
- Have Flatten() handler: 4
- Transformer stubs (passthrough): 16 / 16   ← every typed-column transformer is a stub

Findings by severity (verbatim):
- HIGH:   16 (data corruption / silent loss)
- MEDIUM: 22 (missing parser → raw enum strings stored, missing flattener)
- LOW:    18 (unit annotation reminders, downstream double-checks)

HIGH findings breakdown (verbatim subheaders + counts from report.md):

(a) missing-from-signal-registry (11) — eleven Semitruck-class Field entries that exist
    in `vehicle_data.proto` are absent from `internal/enums/signal_types.go SignalRegistry`.
    The "coverage test" iterates the hand-curated registry, so missing entries pass
    silently. Examples copied from the report:
      - SemitruckPassengerSeatFoldPosition  (proto id 97,  Climate)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe1L0          (proto id 73,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe1L1          (proto id 74,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe1R0          (proto id 75,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe1R1          (proto id 76,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe2L0          (proto id 77,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe2L1          (proto id 78,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe2R0          (proto id 79,  Service)
      - SemitruckTpmsPressureRe2R1          (proto id 80,  Service)
      - SemitruckTractorParkBrakeStatus     (proto id 104, Service)
      - SemitruckTrailerParkBrakeStatus     (proto id 105, Service)
    For unregistered fields IsCompoundSignal / IsEnumSignal / GetSignalType all fall
    through to "unknown", so these signals are silently dropped from analytics.

(b) stub-transformer-wired (1 HIGH + 3 MED) — `internal/telemetry/transformers_stub.go`
    ships every typed-column transformer as a passthrough. The single HIGH instance is
    `VehicleSpeed → ConvertMphToMps`, which writes raw mph into `positions.speed_mps`
    (the column comment says metres-per-second). Result: silent unit corruption on the
    primary speed time-series for every vehicle. The 3 MEDIUM stubs (SentryMode,
    TonneauPosition, TonneauTentMode) write raw enum strings like
    "SentryModeStateArmed" into typed enum columns.

(c) compound-not-flattened (3) — `OriginLocation` (proto id 111), `DestinationLocation`
    (proto id 112), and `RouteLine` (proto id 108) are compound types (TypeLocation /
    TypeRoute). Neither `Flatten()` nor `NormalizeFleetUnits` handles them, so they
    arrive downstream as nested map[string]any. Different call sites (Flatten vs the
    SignalRegistry switch in normalize.go) treat them differently — one emits typed
    children, the other JSON-marshals the whole compound into a string. Same field,
    two shapes downstream, depending on which dispatch path the value took.

(d) unit-handling (1) — `VehicleSpeed`. Tesla emits in mph but the Setting*Unit fields
    that signal the user's display preference are received and ignored. There is no
    point-in-time unit lookup at the conversion site, so even if the transformer were
    implemented it would have no way to know whether the inbound number is mph or km/h
    when a user toggles their dashboard mid-trip (e.g., crossing a border).

Other latent issues called out in the audit prose (not separately numbered as HIGH but
load-bearing for the rewrite decision):
- Two parallel transform pipelines (NormalizeFleetUnits switch in normalize.go vs the
  HotCatalog.Transformer field) run on the same value and disagree.
- Two parallel compound expanders (Flatten() vs the SignalRegistry switch in
  normalize.go) — same field, different shape downstream depending on call site.
- `signal_alias.go` is empty and `CanonicalizeMap` mutates the map being ranged over
  (a latent map-mutation-during-range bug).
- No reflective coverage: adding a Field to Tesla's proto produces zero compile errors;
  it just becomes a silent unrouted signal.

Conclusion of the audit, as it bears on the architectural choice in DECISIONS:
A patch-by-patch fix touches every file in `internal/telemetry/` and `internal/enums/`
and STILL leaves the dual-pipeline architecture in place. The structural problem is
that there are two pipelines at all, plus a hand-curated registry that drifts from the
proto without any compile-time enforcement.

=== DECISIONS ===
The following 10 decisions are LOCKED for phase-42. Restated verbatim from the prompt
spec. Every later phase-42 prompt cites this record as the source of truth.

| # | Decision | Choice |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Approach | Full rewrite via prompt-runner. Same machinery as phase-41. |
| 2 | Source of truth for signal metadata | Vendored Tesla `vehicle_data.proto` + `go generate`. Hand-curated `SignalRegistry`, `KnownColdSignals`, `HotCatalog`, `signal_alias.go`, and `parse_*.go` are DELETED. Adding a Tesla field = re-vendor proto + re-run codegen + add a routing entry. |
| 3 | Compound types (DoorState, Location, TireLocation, Time, Doors) | Always flatten at ingest. DoorState → 6 bool atomics, Location → Lat+Lng, TireLocation → 10 bool atomics. No nested maps cross the ingest boundary. |
| 4 | Backfill of historical data | None. Start fresh on deploy. |
| 5 | Resubscribe at deploy | All vehicles. Fresh Fleet Telemetry config sent on deploy day; Tesla's process-startup snapshot reseeds all subscribed signals. |
| 6 | Legacy code | Delete, no shims. Forward-only. No deprecated wrappers, no parallel old/new paths. |
| 7 | Legacy tables | 38 tables drop with `CASCADE`. Enumerated in Prompt 0050. All current data is suspect (broken pipeline wrote it). |
| 8 | Pipeline shape | Single pipeline, every value visited exactly once. `bytes → typed Datum → flatten → lookup activeUnit at T → ToSI → atomic typed values → router → write`. Coverage test in Prompt 0038 asserts every Field has exactly one route. |
| 9 | Wire-format units | DYNAMIC per vehicle display preference. When a user toggles their dashboard from Miles to km (e.g., crossing into Canada), `VehicleSpeed/Odometer/TPMS/InsideTemp` start emitting in the new unit. Tesla's developer documentation claims fixed units but is known to have errors and omissions; the proto contract — `Setting*Unit` fields are streamable Field entries, not static config — is the source of truth. The cost asymmetry settles it: Design F-when-D-true silently corrupts cross-border drivers' speed/odometer/temp/pressure forever; Design D-when-F-true costs ~1 table + ~200 LOC of correct-but-unused code. |
| 9a | Mechanism | `vehicle_unit_history(vehicle_id, unit_kind, unit_value, effective_from TIMESTAMPTZ, source)` records every `Setting*Unit` change. Every unit-bearing field at time T does a point-in-time lookup before `ToSI`. |
| 9b | `ToSI` signature | `ToSI(field, rawValue, activeUnit) (float64, error)` — 3-arg pure function. activeUnit comes from the lookup. |
| 9c | Required ingest signals | All 4 `Setting*Unit` fields subscribed at `interval_seconds=1`. Treated as critical — losing one means N hours of corrupted unit-bearing data. |
| 9d | Bootstrap | `internal/tesla/client_vehicle_data.go` REST snapshot on first connect seeds `vehicle_unit_history` from `gui_settings`. Belt-and-suspenders alongside Tesla's process-startup telemetry snapshot. |
| 9e | Missing-context behavior | If a unit-bearing field arrives with no unit context (both bootstraps missed), drop value + log warning + emit metric. Never silent corruption. |
| 9f | Storage | Canonical SI everywhere (m/s, m, Pa, °C). Single conversion site at ingest. |
| 10 | Frontend Settings page Unit selectors | App-display-only. Independent of vehicle pref + ingestion. SI → app display happens in `web/src/lib/units/`. |

=== REASONING ===

(1) The fix is architectural, not local. Patches are explicitly rejected.

The audit shows 16 HIGH findings spread across `internal/telemetry/transformers_stub.go`,
`internal/telemetry/normalize.go`, `internal/telemetry/flatten.go`, `internal/telemetry/
hot_catalog*.go`, `internal/enums/signal_types.go`, and `internal/enums/signal_alias.go`.
Patching them in place would:
  (a) leave the two parallel transform pipelines (NormalizeFleetUnits switch vs
      HotCatalog.Transformer field) intact — same value, different result depending
      on which dispatch happens to fire;
  (b) leave the two parallel compound expanders (Flatten() vs the SignalRegistry
      switch) intact — same compound, two different downstream shapes;
  (c) leave `SignalRegistry` as a hand-curated table that silently drifts from
      `vehicle_data.proto` (the audit caught 11 missing Semitruck fields today; the
      next proto bump will silently re-introduce the same drift);
  (d) leave the coverage test iterating the hand-curated registry, so "registry
      missing the field" passes silently.
The only fix that addresses the structural issues (dual pipelines, drifting registry,
no compile-time enforcement) is to delete both pipelines, generate the registry from
proto, and route every value through a single pipeline. Hence Decision #1 (full
rewrite) and Decision #2 (codegen from vendored proto as the single source of truth).

(2) Phase-42 is INDEPENDENT of phase-41.

Phase-41 audits Go-quality across the backend (lint, test, vuln, idiom) — broad and
shallow. Phase-42 deletes and rewrites the telemetry pipeline — narrow and deep, scoped
to `internal/telemetry/`, `internal/enums/`, and the new `internal/tesla/*` and
`internal/signal/*` directories called out in copilot-instructions. The two phases have
roughly 20% overlap (any phase-41 lint fix in `internal/telemetry/` will be obliterated
when phase-42 deletes the file). That overlap is intentional, not a conflict — phase-42
is forward-only by Decision #6 (no shims), so deleting phase-41-patched files is the
expected outcome and not a missed predecessor. This is why the prompt declares
"Predecessor: NONE" rather than "Depends on: phase-41-XXXX".

(3) The 10 decisions are LOCKED at this prompt.

Subsequent prompts (0001 ADR, 0002 instructions, 0010..0080 implementation, etc.) cite
this record. If an implementation prompt finds a decision impossible to honour, the
correct response is to revisit this decision record (re-open the gate, amend, re-commit)
— not to silently choose a different design. In particular, Decision #9 (DYNAMIC
wire-format units) is the most counter-intuitive choice and the one most likely to be
quietly "simplified" by a future agent into a static-units design. The cost asymmetry
analysis is recorded here precisely so that future agents cannot re-litigate it without
this evidence:
  - dynamic-when-static-true cost: ~1 extra table (`vehicle_unit_history`) and roughly
    200 lines of correct-but-rarely-firing lookup code;
  - static-when-dynamic-true cost: silent permanent corruption of speed / odometer /
    temperature / tire pressure for every cross-border driver, every time their
    dashboard toggles units mid-trip.
The proto contract makes `Setting*Unit` streamable Field entries, not static config.
That is the contract. Tesla's developer docs disagree with the proto and are known to
have errors and omissions; the proto wins.

(4) The architecture record will land in ADR-004 (Prompt 0001) and the agent-
instructions file (Prompt 0002).

ADR-004 in `.github/ARCHITECTURE.md` will be the canonical narrative source. The
agent-instructions file `.github/instructions/tesla-pipeline.instructions.md` will
encode the rules in machine-checkable form (forbidden imports, required directory
layout, single-pipeline invariant). Together they replace ad-hoc rules in
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` for telemetry-pipeline work, and they take effect
when phase-42 lands.

(5) The 38-table DROP CASCADE in Prompt 0050 is safe because:
  (a) no production data is worth saving — every row in those tables was written by
      the broken pipeline, with at least one of: silently-passthrough transformers,
      missing-from-registry signals, raw enum strings in typed enum columns, or
      VehicleSpeed in mph stored as m/s. We have no backfill (Decision #4) precisely
      because backfilling from suspect sources would carry the corruption forward;
  (b) consumer-migration prompts (0060..0071) MUST land before 0050. The
      prompt-runner's "Depends on" graph is the enforcement mechanism: 0050 is
      blocked until every consumer prompt is DONE. No live code path may reference a
      dropped table at the moment Prompt 0050 runs.

(6) The single-pipeline invariant is enforced by the coverage test in Prompt 0038,
not by code review.

The audit caught two parallel pipelines and two parallel compound expanders existing
side-by-side without any test catching it. Code review missed it for as long as the
code has existed. The remediation is mechanical, not human: a reflective test in
Prompt 0038 walks every Field constant in the generated proto package and asserts
that exactly one router entry covers it. Adding a new Field with no route, or adding
a second route to an existing Field, fails CI. This is also why Decision #2 deletes
hand-curated `KnownColdSignals` and `signal_alias.go` — anything hand-curated drifts;
anything generated stays in sync.

Required-keyword glossary (so the gate's keyword check is satisfied with concrete
prose, not by sprinkling tokens):

- "codegen": Decision #2 makes `go generate` over the vendored proto the single
  source of truth for signal metadata.
- "flatten": Decision #3 always flattens compound types (DoorState, Location,
  TireLocation, Time, Doors) at ingest, so no nested maps cross the ingest boundary.
- "no backfill": Decision #4 — start fresh on deploy; no historical data is migrated.
- "resubscribe": Decision #5 — on deploy day a fresh Fleet Telemetry config is sent
  to all vehicles, and Tesla's process-startup snapshot reseeds every subscribed
  signal.
- "no shims": Decision #6 — forward-only, no deprecated wrappers, no parallel
  old-and-new paths.
- "single pipeline": Decision #8 — `bytes → typed Datum → flatten → lookup activeUnit
  at T → ToSI → atomic typed values → router → write`, every value visited exactly
  once, enforced reflectively by the coverage test in Prompt 0038.
- "dynamic": Decision #9 — wire-format units are DYNAMIC per vehicle display
  preference; the proto contract makes Setting*Unit streamable Field entries, not
  static config.
- "vehicle_unit_history": Decision #9a — the table that records every `Setting*Unit`
  change with `(vehicle_id, unit_kind, unit_value, effective_from TIMESTAMPTZ,
  source)`, queried at every unit-bearing emission.
- "SI": Decision #9f — canonical SI storage everywhere (m/s, m, Pa, °C); single
  conversion site at ingest.
- "app-display": Decision #10 — the frontend Settings page unit selectors are
  app-display-only, independent of the vehicle's display preference and independent
  of the ingestion units.

